WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:06.639
So that was a bit of a whistle-stop tour on the kind of fundamental building blocks of getting gameplay working

2
00:00:06.639 --> 00:00:12.859
But one thing we also really need to touch on is garbage collection as already mentioned unreal is a garbage collected

3
00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:19.960
Engine it's built on top of C++, which obviously isn't garbage collected, but that functionality has been implemented over the top

4
00:00:21.260 --> 00:00:23.260
as mentioned earlier the

5
00:00:23.539 --> 00:00:28.280
Most of the unreal garbage collection system is managed through the reflection macros

6
00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:35.259
So when if we want something garbage collected it essentially needs to be marked up either as a uclass, a ustruct or a uproperty

7
00:00:36.299 --> 00:00:42.479
The way garbage collection actually works is that unreal keeps a root set of u objects

8
00:00:42.479 --> 00:00:45.719
That acts as the base for the garbage collected kind of iterative searches

9
00:00:45.719 --> 00:00:49.460
And we'll touch on the root set in just a second to go into any more detail about how that works

10
00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:57.759
Actors can be explicitly destroyed and that's how we easily remove things from that root set and once an actor has been destroyed

11
00:00:57.759 --> 00:01:02.899
It'll get cleaned up by the garbage collector at the next garbage collection. This also means that any

12
00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:05.640
properties or any

13
00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:07.599
components on that actor

14
00:01:07.599 --> 00:01:10.620
That aren't already being referenced by other things will get cleaned up

15
00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:15.280
And actors own components will get automatically garbage collected at that point, too

16
00:01:15.719 --> 00:01:20.000
So just going into a little bit about how the root set actually works like we said

17
00:01:20.319 --> 00:01:26.319
Unreal maintains a kind of core root set of objects. You can add objects to this root set yourself if you need to

18
00:01:27.319 --> 00:01:30.719
But if we were to kind of step through a garbage collection flow

19
00:01:31.159 --> 00:01:37.359
The way unreal begins is it assumes that every object outside of that root set is unreachable

20
00:01:37.359 --> 00:01:39.859
So it marks everything as needing garbage collection

21
00:01:39.859 --> 00:01:45.019
It then goes through a series of iterative searches to try and build up connections where it can

22
00:01:45.099 --> 00:01:47.519
To see what is and is not reachable

23
00:01:47.719 --> 00:01:49.319
So in this example here

24
00:01:49.319 --> 00:01:55.760
We have an object which references down a chain and we can see now all of these objects are reachable and therefore

25
00:01:55.840 --> 00:02:01.359
None of them would get garbage collected if at the end of this iterative search any of these objects

26
00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:06.959
Didn't have other objects referencing them. Then that would be marked up for garbage collection and cleaned up

27
00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:11.159
If at some point we were to break one of these connections

28
00:02:11.159 --> 00:02:17.300
So say for example objects one here was our level of level world and we've destroyed an actor

29
00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:18.319
So object three is an actor

30
00:02:18.319 --> 00:02:23.520
we've destroyed it that would be breaking its connection with the world and therefore the root set and

31
00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:28.919
Therefore both the actor and the actors component would get garbage collected

32
00:02:30.460 --> 00:02:32.460
so it's not just

33
00:02:32.979 --> 00:02:39.520
The reflected types that can get garbage collected. It's not just a U class or a U struct

34
00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:45.699
We can do it in another way if we have non U objects that we need to

35
00:02:46.340 --> 00:02:48.659
Have still handle garbage collection

36
00:02:48.699 --> 00:02:55.259
We can inherit from the FGC object class and that just implements this function of add referenced objects

37
00:02:55.259 --> 00:03:02.840
And that way you can add in references to U objects and any type that inherits from that to a non U object class

38
00:03:04.379 --> 00:03:10.139
You just need to make sure that you implement those functions and within that function you reference each one of the objects that you need

39
00:03:10.139 --> 00:03:11.060
to be

40
00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:13.060
Seen by the garbage collector

41
00:03:14.569 --> 00:03:17.610
When working with garbage collection, there's a couple of key things to bear in mind

42
00:03:17.969 --> 00:03:22.490
So whenever we're talking about U objects that manipulation isn't thread safe

43
00:03:22.650 --> 00:03:27.370
It's highly highly recommended that we only manipulate and access

44
00:03:27.849 --> 00:03:29.849
U objects on the game thread

45
00:03:29.849 --> 00:03:32.330
It is possible to do it on other threads

46
00:03:32.490 --> 00:03:39.050
But we need to use proper use of synchronization techniques and primitives to make sure that we don't get parallel programming errors

47
00:03:39.569 --> 00:03:41.969
So be cautious because that can lead to performance

48
00:03:42.930 --> 00:03:45.189
hitches

49
00:03:45.509 --> 00:03:51.280
The garbage collector can't see raw pointers inside U objects. So even though the reflection

50
00:03:52.159 --> 00:03:57.520
The unreal reflection system looks inside U classes and U structs if you have raw pointers

51
00:03:57.919 --> 00:04:00.159
They're just not going to be seen by garbage collection

52
00:04:00.159 --> 00:04:05.819
So you either need a U object for U property markup, or you can also use the shared pointer library

53
00:04:05.819 --> 00:04:08.219
Which we'll get on to in a moment

54
00:04:08.219 --> 00:04:10.819
Last of all, we can mark objects for pending kill

55
00:04:11.060 --> 00:04:16.939
Which is essentially our manual way of marking sending an object for garbage collection. Actors can be destroyed

56
00:04:16.939 --> 00:04:18.740
That's kind of a distinct thing

57
00:04:18.740 --> 00:04:25.480
But generally we can mark an object up for pending kill the pending being the key word there because when you do that

58
00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:27.500
It's not instantly

59
00:04:27.620 --> 00:04:33.860
Deleted from the root set that simply breaks that connection and the next time garbage collection actually gets run

60
00:04:34.259 --> 00:04:36.779
That's when the objects will be removed from memory

61
00:04:36.779 --> 00:04:43.300
So it's only at the point of garbage collection pending kill simply says this will get garbage collected the next time we run garbage collection
